# Harness project

This repo is a file agent: an OpenAI-compatible chat loop, tools confined to one workspace, and a JSONL trace in that workspace's `session.log`.

- `src/harnh3x/config.py` holds `PROVIDER`, `API_KEY`, `API_URL`, `MAX_TOKENS`, `CONTEXT_LIMIT`, `MODEL`, `MAX_TURNS`, `SUBAGENT_MAX_TURNS`, `COMPACT_AT`, `MAX_TOOL_RESULT`, the fetch/search/feed limits, `SHELL_TIMEOUT`, `SHELL_MAX_TIMEOUT`, and `SHELL_MAX_OUTPUT`, the file listing and search caps, `PDF_MAX_PAGES`, `PREVIEW_ROWS`, `MEMORY_FILE`, `CUSTOM_SKILLS_DIR`, `PLAN_FILE`, `TRANSCRIPT_FILE`, `WIKIPEDIA_LANG`, and `BASE_DIR` (default `~/.harnh3x`, expanded when it is absolute or starts with `~`). Sessions live in `BASE_DIR/workspaces`. `MEMORY_FILE` is `BASE_DIR/memory.md`, shared across sessions. User skills live in `BASE_DIR/skills` (`CUSTOM_SKILLS_DIR`). `~/.config/harnh3x/harnh3x.yaml` (or `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/harnh3x/harnh3x.yaml`) is YAML and can set the API key, API URL, and those tunables except `MEMORY_FILE`, `PLAN_FILE`, and `TRANSCRIPT_FILE`. A non-empty process environment variable overrides the file for those scalars, and a working-directory `.env` fills variables that are not already set. `HARNH3X_CONFIG` selects another file; an empty value skips it. `mcp` is a mapping of stdio servers (`command`, `args`, `env`, `timeout`, `description`, `instructions`, `include`, `exclude`). Environment variables do not override `mcp`. If `harnh3x.conf` exists and `harnh3x.yaml` does not, startup exits and names both files. `MCP_SERVERS` holds the validated servers. `provider` is `openai` (default) or `anthropic`. For `openai`, startup exits when `openai_api_key` or `openai_api_url` is unset; `OPENAI_API_KEY` and `OPENAI_BASE_URL` override those. For `anthropic`, the key is `anthropic_api_key` and `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` overrides the file; `openai_api_key` and `openai_api_url` are ignored, and the URL is `https://api.anthropic.com`. `max_tokens` defaults to 8192. `context_limit` is optional and applies to both providers; when it is unset, Claude compaction uses 200000 and the OpenAI path asks Ollama's `/api/show`. `--yolo` stays a launch flag.
- `src/harnh3x/failures.py` holds the fixed answers a turn returns when it cannot finish (timeout, unreachable API, rejection prefix, max turns, empty answer) and `is_failure_answer`; the OpenAI and Anthropic paths both use those strings.
- `src/harnh3x/anthropic_api.py` translates the stored transcript to the Anthropic Messages API and maps the stream back into the same assistant message. Streamed requests mark prompt-cache breakpoints on the system prompt and the last two user messages; the summary call does not. Cached input tokens count toward `context_tokens`. `get_client()` returns that client when `provider` is `anthropic`.
- `src/harnh3x/prompt.py` builds the system prompt: date and OS, a one-line workspace listing, the saved memory keys, the prompt contract, and the skill menu. It accepts `now` for tests, `yolo` to add the unsandboxed-session sentence, `project` to name a granted folder and point code changes there, `rules` for that folder's Cursor rules, and `coding` to replace the assistant contract with a coding-agent prompt. The coding prompt says that @path is a file or folder in the project to read before answering, and that a trailing slash is a folder.
- `src/harnh3x/rules.py` reads `<project>/.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc` only in a coding session. `alwaysApply` rules go in the system prompt. `globs` may be a comma-separated line or a YAML list; a matching file tool appends that rule once. A symlink that resolves outside the project is skipped. A bad frontmatter block is skipped.
- `src/harnh3x/tools/` defines the tool schema and the sandbox. Each skill module exports its schemas and functions. `TOOLS` is the full domain catalog assembled from those modules. `tools/scope.py` adds the session's sandbox, yolo, and project sentence to each sent schema.
- `src/harnh3x/skills/` holds `SkillRegistry` plus one `SKILL.md` per skill: `file-ops`, `shell`, `python`, `coding`, `research`, `reference`, `http`, `documents`, `data`, `memory`, `tasks`, and `hardware-diagnosis`. A skill may live in a category subdirectory (`skills/<category>/<name>/SKILL.md`, any depth). Each `SKILL.md` starts with YAML frontmatter: `name` matches the folder and `description` is the menu line; `license`, `compatibility`, `metadata`, and `allowed-tools` are optional. `allowed-tools` is a space-separated list of tool names and is the skill's tool list; skills may share a tool. `description` says what the skill does and when to load it, since the menu is all the model sees before loading; the body has no "When to Use" section. The body is freeform Markdown with workflow and reporting conventions, and is returned by `load_skill`. The loop sends `load_skill`, `unload_skill`, `task`, and schemas for loaded skills only, each tool name once; several skills can be loaded at once. Configured MCP servers are extra skills in category `mcp`. `SkillRegistry` starts each one and runs `initialize` and `tools/list` when it is built, and keeps that process for `load_skill`. Their tool schemas come from the server, not from `TOOLS`, and are sent only once the skill is loaded. `src/harnh3x/mcp/` is the stdio client. The menu line is the config `description`, else the server's `serverInfo.description`, else the first line of its `instructions` that is not a heading, else the server name and version, the tool count, and the first five tool names. A server whose tools collide with a built-in tool is dropped with a warning. Its `load_skill` result is the config `description`, the config `instructions`, the server's `initialize` instructions, and its resource list. The child is not kernel-sandboxed. `unload_skill` drops its schemas and leaves the process up. `reset()`, `resume()`, and process exit close it. An MCP skill is omitted when an `http` or `https` URL in its `env` is not accepting connections. A handshake that cannot finish removes that skill from the menu. `SkillRegistry(custom_dir=...)` reads user skills from that directory after the MCP handshakes, with the same layout and frontmatter rules as the built-in skills; `Session` passes `BASE_DIR/skills`. They are `custom=True` and listed under category `custom` (or `custom/<sub>`). Their `allowed-tools` takes built-in names, `server:tool` for one MCP tool, and `server:*` for every tool that server exposes; `execute` routes those MCP tools to that server's client. A malformed custom skill, an unknown tool, an unknown or unavailable server, a tool the server does not offer, or a taken name skips that skill and adds `(location, reason)` to `warnings`. `available_counts()` is `(skills, tools, custom_skills, mcp_servers, mcp_tools)`. `overview()` lists every skill in menu order as `(name, category, loaded, tool_names)`.
- `src/harnh3x/agent/` owns `Session` and `run`. `Session.reset()` starts a new conversation in a new workspace, keeping the model, `yolo`, `project`, `coding`, and `debug`. `Session.resume()` reloads a workspace's `transcript.json`, restores `project` and `coding` from that file, replays skill loads, and refreshes the system prompt. `reset()` and `resume()` clear the set of glob rules already appended. A transcript with no `project` clears any launch grant. A transcript with no `coding`, or with `coding` false, clears a launch coding session. A non-boolean `coding` value fails resume. `coding` is stored in the transcript when the session is a coding session. `yolo` and `debug` stay launch flags and are not stored in the transcript. `reset()` and `resume()` keep `debug`. `src/harnh3x/agent/subagent.py` runs `task`. `explore` is a read-only child loop (`glob`, `grep`, `read_file`, `list_files`, `outline_python`) that returns one answer and cannot modify files or start another subagent. It stops after `SUBAGENT_MAX_TURNS` (default 12) and does not ask to continue.
- `src/harnh3x/cli.py` is the CLI, exposed as the `harnh3x` console script. `--coding PATH` grants sandboxed read, write, and shell access to one existing directory and starts a coding session there. The path must not overlap the session workspace; `/` is rejected. Relative paths and the shell working directory use that folder. The session uses a coding system prompt and that project's Cursor rules. The path and the coding flag are stored in `transcript.json` and restored by `/resume`. Ctrl+N keeps them. Plain output and the header print `Project:` and `Coding` on the next line. `--debug` shows the system prompt once at the start of the session, and again only when that prompt changes, plus the other messages sent on each completion, in cyan panels that keep the last three lines until a click expands them. It is not stored. Ctrl+N and `/resume` keep it. Plain output prints `Debug` on the line after `Coding`. The status bar shows a `DEBUG` badge. `--answer-only` hides those panels. The summary call is not shown. `--yolo` disables the kernel sandbox (Seatbelt on macOS, Landlock and seccomp on Linux): read tools may open any path, shell and Python may run any program, including sudo, and `run_shell` is `/bin/sh -c`, so pipes, redirects, chaining, and `$` run. HTTP tools may use any host, including localhost and private addresses. File mutation tools stay in the workspace, or in the project when one is granted. A tty session uses Textual and the Dracula palette (`src/harnh3x/tui/`: `text.py` wrapping and panel text, `theme.py` the Dracula palette, `mentions.py` the coding-session @ file and folder list, `transcript.py` the turn and compaction model, `export.py` answer export and clipboard, `panels.py` the transcript widgets, where `Thinking`, `DebugBox`, and `ToolBox` share `CollapsiblePanel`, and `TurnHeading` is the model-round label above a horizontal `Rule`, `pickers.py` the numbered list screens, where the model, resume, and export screens share `NumberedPicker`, `modals.py` the overlays, `status.py` the status bar segments and layout, `app.py` the app and runners; `__init__` re-exports every public name): a header whose title is Harnh3x, then the workspace, the project when one is set, and Coding when the session is a coding session, then the transcript. A ⧉ control beside the workspace path copies that path to the clipboard. A status bar under the prompt, with a blank row between them, is a row of colored badges (`tui/status.py`). `HarnessApp._status_state()` collects a `StatusState`, each function in `SEGMENT_BUILDERS` turns it into at most one `Segment` (`key`, `text`, `style`, `side`, `priority`, `action`), and `layout_segments` lays them out at the bar width; a new badge is one more builder. The left side shows the model, then the context used against the model's limit as `ctx 5k/262k 2%` (`compact_count` formats the numbers, the percentage only when the limit is known; the badge turns yellow at 80% of the `COMPACT_AT` point and red at or past it), then the last completion's cached prompt tokens as `cache 3.6k 92%` (`cache_text`; `cache_share` is the cached count over the previous completion's prompt plus reply, the most that completion could read back, capped at 100%, so new tool output does not lower it; no percentage on the first completion; yellow under 50%; hidden when the server reports no cached count; a click (action `cache`) opens `CacheScreen`, whose `cache_details` text gives the last request's read, written, reusable, and prompt counts, says the ctx badge is that prompt plus the reply, then the session reuse rate and the number of requests under 50% reused, from `session.cache_rounds`), then `YOLO` in bright yellow when the sandbox is off, then `DEBUG` in cyan when `--debug` is set. The right side shows `skills N`, with `+N` for custom skills when there are any, `tools N`, `mcp N · M tools` when MCP servers are accepting connections, a red `⚠ N` when a configured MCP server or custom skill is unavailable, and the package version last. When the badges do not fit, the lowest-priority one is dropped first: version, then tools, skills, mcp, cache, `DEBUG`, context, model, the warning badge, and `YOLO` last. Ctrl+W, or a click on the `⚠ N` badge (its `action` is `warnings`), opens the reasons. A click on the skills or tools badge (action `skills`) opens `SkillsScreen`, which lists the loaded skills, then the ones not loaded, each with its category and tool names, from `SkillRegistry.overview()`; Escape closes it. A click on the model badge (action `model`), when no turn is running, calls `list_models` on a worker thread and opens `SwitchModelScreen`: the `ModelScreen` list, filterable, with the current model marked `(current)` and highlighted, and Escape to cancel. Picking another model sets `session.model`, clears `context_limit`, and fetches the new limit; a failed or empty list is a toast instead. `HarnessApp` takes the listing function as `models=`. The boxes the status bar opens (warnings, skills, cache details, model choice) mix in `BackdropCloses`: a click on the dimmed area outside the box closes it like Escape. A click elsewhere on the bar does nothing. Those marks stay after Ctrl+N and `/resume`. Plain stdout still prints `YOLO — sandbox off` before the workspace and model. `--answer-only` stays the final answer only. The composer sits in a tall box that is clear until it is focused, then uses the secondary color. A `❯` marks the draft and becomes `☰` once it has a newline. Enter submits, and Shift+Enter or Ctrl+J adds a newline; the box grows with its text up to eight rows, then scrolls so the cursor stays in view. Up on its first row and Down on its last row walk the past prompts of this transcript and this run, keeping the draft. Typing `/` shows the matching commands above the composer, and Tab completes the first one. In a coding session, typing `@` opens a filtered list of project files and folders above the composer; Up and Down move through that list. The list shows eight rows and scrolls when the highlight moves past them. Right enters the selected directory and Left returns to its parent, until the project root. Tab or Enter inserts `@path` (a folder ends with `/`), and Escape closes it. While a turn or `/compact` runs, a line above the composer shows a spinner, the phase (waiting, thinking, writing, running a named tool, compacting, stopping), the elapsed time, and `Esc to stop`. Esc sets `session.abort`, kills tool child processes, and when the worker returns clears the abort, adds a `Stopped.` note, and re-enables the composer; the app stays open. When that turn has used `MAX_TURNS` completions and the model is still calling tools, a modal asks to continue for another window of the same size. Enter continues. Escape stops, and the turn ends with `Max turns reached.` While that modal is open the activity line shows `Max turns reached` and omits `Esc to stop`. Ctrl+Q and `/exit` quit and stop the running request. Ctrl+N and `/new` start a new session in a new workspace and refresh the header path. Ctrl+H and `/details` hide or show every thinking box, tool box, debug box, compaction note, and turn heading, including ones added while they are hidden. In Terminal.app (`TERM_PROGRAM` is `Apple_Terminal`), `translate_legacy_key` in `text.py` turns Backspace carrying `\x08` into Ctrl+H and a Ctrl+W with no character (Option+Backspace with Meta on) into Alt+Backspace before bindings run; other terminals pass keys through unchanged. Each model completion shows `Turn N`, starting at 1 for that request, above a horizontal `Rule`. Ctrl+H hides that heading with the other trace panels. User messages carry an accent bar and render Markdown; an answer that `is_failure_answer` accepts carries a red bar; notes are dim and command errors are red. Copy and export results show as toasts. `src/harnh3x/commands.py` is the one slash-command table: `COMMANDS` names each command, whether it takes an argument, and its usage line; `help_lines()` builds the Commands block of both help texts; `dispatch()` routes a parsed command to the caller's handler and reports unknown names and wrong argument shapes. The plain prompt and the composer both use it; `parse_command` in `harnh3x.agent` splits the typed text. `/help` opens a scrollable box of commands and hotkeys that Escape closes. The pickers are bordered modal boxes. `/resume` opens a list of past sessions and restores that transcript; typed text that is not a number filters that list. `/compact` forces a context compaction. `/export PATH` writes one answer, or every answer, to a markdown file. More than one answer asks which response, or all of them. The file is the answer text only. Icons at the bottom right of a response copy it or write it to a markdown file; the path box starts with `answer-YYYYMMDD-HHMM.md` and names the directory a relative path uses. The path may be outside the workspace; a relative path uses the project directory when one is granted, otherwise the working directory. Each model round's tool calls share a green bordered box, in the order the API produced them. A call appears when it starts, as `name  key=value`, marked `…` until `session.on_tool_done` reports its result, then `✓`, or `✗` with the first line of an `Error` result; replayed calls have no mark. That box keeps the last three lines, with each value cut at 80 characters, until a click expands it to every line and the full values; a box with a cut value can expand even when it fits in three lines. A `task` call also mounts a subagent box titled with the subagent type and its description. The child's turn headings, thinking, and tool calls render inside that box, with the same marks, and do not join the parent's thinking or tool boxes. That box keeps the last three lines until a click expands it. Its border and title, and the borders and titles of the boxes inside it, are purple, and the box is indented from the left. Ctrl+H hides that box with the other trace panels. A click on any collapsible box leaves focus in the composer; a focused box toggles with Enter or Space. Thinking and the answer update while the completion streams; each thinking box keeps the last three lines until a click expands it. While output is arriving, the transcript stays at the bottom until the user scrolls up, and scrolling back to the bottom follows again. A context compaction shows Compacting context… while the summary runs, then a Context compacted note, and the agent answer is rendered as Markdown. One-shot on a tty starts that view before the request so tokens are visible as they arrive, and waits for Enter to close when the turn included reasoning. Plain stdout writes reasoning and the answer as the tokens arrive. `/resume` reprints a finished transcript in that same order, with the collapsed thinking panel for a finished reasoning block. Piped input and `--answer-only` stay plain stdout. `--model` with no name uses the Textual list on a tty and a numbered prompt otherwise. Escape closes that list and exits. When the configured default is not in the provider's model list, startup uses that same chooser and says that model is missing from this provider. An explicit `--model NAME` is used as given. A list that cannot be fetched leaves the configured default in place. Tests live under `tests/`, one `test_*.py` per subsystem (`test_agent_loop`, `test_subagent`, `test_session`, `test_compaction`, `test_skills`, `test_cli`, `test_tui_helpers`, `test_tui_app`, `test_file_ops`, `test_shell`, `test_project`, `test_research`, `test_http`, `test_documents_data`, `test_memory_tasks_prompt`, `test_config`, `test_anthropic`, `test_mcp`, `test_system_info`, `test_tools_core`); fakes used by more than one file are in `tests/helpers.py`.
- Export new public symbols from `src/harnh3x/__init__.py` `__all__`.

Poetry with `package-mode = true` and a `poetry-core` build backend so pip and pipx install the `harnh3x` script. Pytest uses `pythonpath = ["src"]`. Format with Ruff. Do not commit `src/.env`.

A new tool, or a looser path or shell check, needs a matching test.

```python
# BAD: a second model constant next to the loop
MODEL = "other"

# GOOD: change the constant in src/harnh3x/config.py
from harnh3x.config import MODEL
```
